<TopicPage slug="subjects/urban-planning/foundational/planning-history-and-theory">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Urban Planning. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only
    experience of being in a city, any city: walking streets that feel alive, crossing
    roads that feel hostile, hearing about a development that neighbours opposed. That
    experience is the raw material; this lesson gives it a history and a theory.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace how modern planning grew out of sanitary reform, and name the movements that built the profession — City Beautiful, garden cities, zoning, modernism;</li>
    <li>tell the postwar cautionary tales — Brasília, the New Towns, American urban renewal — and say what each one taught the field;</li>
    <li>state the rational-comprehensive model, and explain the two critiques (Lindblom's incrementalism, Jacobs's street-level observation) that demoted it to one tool among many;</li>
    <li>describe the communicative turn and audit any "participation" exercise with Arnstein's ladder;</li>
    <li>apply the just-city tests — equity, diversity, democracy — and explain why they trade off;</li>
    <li>reason through a planning-ethics dilemma using the professional codes' actual demands.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a plan, study or consultation appears, we ask the field's oldest
    question — <em>who gets to decide, and who benefits?</em> — before we ask whether it is
    well designed.
  </p>

  <h2>The scene that starts planning theory: a zoning hearing</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a crowded school hall. A developer wants to replace a car park with an
    apartment building. The planner presents a traffic study; neighbours arrive with
    printed objections; a young renter stands up and asks what the building would cost,
    and nobody on the panel can answer. Every position in the room belongs to one of the
    field's three great traditions. The traffic study is the <em>rational model</em>
    speaking — goals, data, alternatives, evaluation. The meeting itself is
    <em>communicative practice</em> — planning as structured public argument. And the
    renter's unanswered question is the <em>just city</em> — who benefits, and who is
    absent from the room?
  </p>
  <p>
    Planning is the deliberate attempt to shape how cities grow, and its history is a
    century-long argument about <em>who</em> gets to do the shaping, and for whom. A
    planner without all three vocabularies is fluent in only part of their own job — which
    is why this lesson tells the story in order: how the profession formed, how its
    rational self-image rose and fell, and how communication and justice rebuilt it.
  </p>

  <h2>The evolution of cities and planning</h2>
  <p>
    Modern planning crystallised between 1890 and 1930, but its seed was sanitation. The
    industrial city of the 1840s was a mortality machine — cholera traced to contaminated
    wells, slum death rates that scandalised statisticians — and the first urban
    intervention that mattered was engineering: sewers, piped water, paved streets. The
    sanitary insight, that urban form determines urban health, became the profession's
    charter, which is why planning still shares intellectual DNA with public health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Baron Haussmann's Paris (1853–1870) remains the grandest statement of the top-down
    model: boulevards, aqueducts, sewers and parks imposed by decree, displacing thousands
    — magnificent, and a standing argument about whom such magnificence served. Three
    overlapping movements then built the profession. The <strong>City Beautiful</strong>
    movement (its showcase the 1893 Chicago World's Fair) treated monumental design as
    civic reform: if the city looked noble, citizens would be ennobled — a theory visible
    today in every beaux-arts civic centre. Ebenezer Howard's <strong>garden cities</strong>
    (1898) proposed the opposite scale: small self-contained towns of about 30,000, ringed
    by permanent greenbelt, balancing town and country — built at Letchworth (1903) and
    Welwyn, and ancestor to the entire new-towns movement. Daniel Burnham's monumental
    1909 plan for Chicago ("make no little plans") brought comprehensive citywide planning
    to America. The same era's scientific-management spirit — the efficiency movement
    reshaping factories — pushed city governments toward expert administration, giving
    planning its civil-service home.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Zoning</strong> gave planning its everyday legal machinery. New York's 1916
    resolution (prompted in part by the Equitable Building, a tower walling out its
    neighbours' light) separated uses and shaped building bulk, and the US Supreme Court
    constitutionalised the device in <em>Euclid v. Ambler</em> (1926). The modernist
    <strong>Athens Charter</strong> (CIAM, 1933) consolidated a functionalist orthodoxy —
    separate the city into dwelling, work, recreation and circulation — which dominated
    postwar rebuilding and produced the towers-in-parks estates, urban-renewal clearances
    and highway cuts that a generation of critics, led from the sidewalk by Jacobs,
    dismantled.
  </p>
  <p>
    The postwar decades tested the orthodoxy at full scale, and the results are the field's
    shared case studies. Brasília (inaugurated 1960) built a modernist capital entire —
    monumental axes, superblocks, functions strictly separated — visually stunning and, its
    critics note, a city hard to walk and hostile to street life. Britain's New Towns Act
    (1946) delivered Howard's scale-up: dozens of planned towns absorbing postwar
    population, largely judged a quiet success. American urban renewal became the
    definitive cautionary tale: federally funded clearance of "slums" that were often
    functioning (if poor) communities, replaced by housing projects and highways whose
    disruption of social networks outlasted any building. James Baldwin's 1963 phrase for
    what Black communities experienced — <em>removal</em>, dressed as renewal — is still
    quoted in the literature. Planning's post-1970s turn toward preservation, community
    development corporations and consultation is the direct institutional scar tissue.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="key moments in planning thought, 1853–2010" widget="timeline" config={upTl} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> read the timeline's tags as a seesaw. The first half
    is all top-down vision — grand plans and regulation — then a pivot: 1961 and 1969 are
    critiques of exactly that tradition, and the final entries reframe the whole field
    around who decides. The dates also show how young this profession is: every entry fits
    inside the lifetime of a city's oldest trees.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> zoning is a neutral safety regulation, a technical map
  of what belongs where. Historically it has been at least as much a sorting machine:
  minimum lot sizes and single-family-only districts — legal in the US for a century —
  function to exclude cheaper housing, and were often designed to, in living memory
  alongside explicitly discriminatory tools like redlining (the 1930s federal maps that
  graded minority neighbourhoods as unbankable, whose effects persist in wealth and tree
  cover to this day). The profession's current debates — upzoning, missing-middle housing,
  exclusionary suburbia — are the direct descendants of this history, which is why the
  just-city theorists insist that planning instruments are never value-neutral.</p>

  <h2>The rational model, and the critiques that demoted it</h2>
  <p>
    The mid-century textbook answer to "how should planners decide?" was the
    <em>rational-comprehensive model</em>: define goals, gather data, generate all feasible
    alternatives, evaluate each against criteria, choose the optimum, implement. It is the
    same logical skeleton that business and government management use, and for some
    decisions it genuinely is the right tool — a new water treatment plant has a knowable
    goal, measurable alternatives and a public interest no hearing can dissolve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three theories, in one ascent: rational → communicative → just" widget="equationStep" config={ptCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are not rivals on equal terms — they
    are a chronological ascent. Each layer absorbed, rather than deleted, the one before
    it: the critics kept cost-benefit analysis as a tool, communicative planning kept the
    analysis but changed who is in the room, and the just city kept the conversation but
    asked what it distributes. When you meet any planning decision, work out which step
    the deciders are standing on.
  </p>

  <p>
    As a general theory, though, the rational model had two blind spots, exposed in two
    famous critiques. Charles Lindblom (1959) argued that real decisions are made
    incrementally — successive limited comparisons, "muddling through" — because
    comprehensive analysis is impossible, values conflict, and politics negotiates. Jane
    Jacobs (1961) showed with close street observation that the city is an organic system
    whose ordered vitality — mixed uses, short blocks, old buildings, dense sidewalk life —
    is exactly what functionalist planning deleted. The rational model survived both
    critiques, but demoted: one instrument in the kit, valid when goals are agreed and
    effects measurable.
  </p>
  <p>
    A metro-line appraisal shows the model working as designed: goals named (ridership,
    emissions, access to jobs), alternatives generated (bus rapid transit, light rail,
    heavy rail, no-build), each evaluated over decades of costs and benefits in a single
    comparable table, the discount rate argued openly, the choice documented. Where goals
    are genuinely shared, this machinery disciplines power rather than serving it — which
    is precisely why its failures elsewhere teach so much.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if we just collect better data, the politics
  disappears." The rational model feeds on this hope, and sometimes the hope is justified —
  for pipes and trains with agreed purposes. But a forecast is advocacy with decimal
  points: the choice of what to measure (traffic speed versus traveller time, property
  value versus housing cost) quietly decides who wins, decades before the hearing. Better
  data sharpens the analysis; it cannot choose the values, because choosing values is the
  politics.</p>

  <h2>The communicative turn: planning as structured argument</h2>
  <p>
    The communicative turn rebuilt the field around the insight that planning is, at
    bottom, structured argument about a shared future. Drawing on Habermas's theory of
    communicative action, theorists such as Patsy Healey and John Forester re-described the
    planner's core skill as convening: bringing stakeholders to the table, brokering
    information so all parties can actually understand it, listening for the power
    dynamics that silence some voices, and legitimating decisions through genuine dialogue
    rather than expert decree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sherry Arnstein's ladder (1969) remains the field's sharpest auditing tool for this
    practice. Participation runs from manipulation and therapy at the bottom, through
    informing and consultation (rungs where citizens hear and are heard but do not
    decide), to partnership, delegated power and citizen control at the top — and too much
    real-world "engagement" lives on the lower rungs while wearing the vocabulary of the
    upper. The practice methods built on this theory are now standard kit: design
    charrettes that run for days instead of one hostile evening, citizens' assemblies that
    pay participants to reason together, participatory budgeting (pioneered in Porto Alegre
    in 1989, now global) that hands real money to real decisions, and third-party
    facilitation when the room's own power dynamics would devour the conversation. Each
    tool is an attempt to move a specific step up Arnstein's ladder.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "participation happened because a meeting happened."
  A well-attended consultation can score low on Arnstein's ladder if the agenda was fixed
  before anyone arrived — the participants were informed, consulted, and then ignored, all
  while the minutes record "public engagement completed". The ladder measures who
  <em>decides</em>, not who attends. A planning body can satisfy every procedural
  requirement and still practice tokenism, which is why the auditors' question is never
  "was there a meeting?" but "what could the participants actually change?"</p>

  <h2>The just city: who gets what, and who decides</h2>
  <p>
    The just-city tradition asks the normative question both predecessors skirted. David
    Harvey's <em>Social Justice and the City</em> (1973) reframed planning as distribution
    under capital — land, rent and whose profit the plan serves — and Henri Lefebvre's
    "right to the city" gave the movement its slogan. Susan Fainstein's <em>The Just
    City</em> (2010), testing New York, London and Amsterdam, settled on three evaluative
    criteria: <strong>equity</strong> (distribution of benefit), <strong>diversity</strong>
    (who can live where) and <strong>democracy</strong> (who decides).
  </p>
  <p>
    The crucial twist is that Fainstein acknowledged the three trade off. A deeply
    democratic process can still produce exclusionary outcomes — the well-organised
    homeowners' meeting — and an equitable outcome can arrive top-down, over the
    residents' objections. Planning theory after 2000 is largely the management of that
    triangle, and it is why no single test — not the study, not the consultation, not the
    equity metric — can certify a plan as good on its own.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning ethics: making the values explicit</h2>
  <p>
    Planning ethics is unusually concrete, because planning decisions redistribute value
    by the million: a single rezoning can transfer enormous wealth between owners, and a
    highway alignment decides which neighbourhood absorbs the noise. The professional
    codes (AICP in the US, the RTPI's in the UK) converge on durable principles: serve the
    public interest while acknowledging it is plural and contested; give affected people a
    genuine voice; protect the interests of those least able to defend them; disclose
    conflicts of interest; and be truthful with data.
  </p>
  <p>
    The recurring dilemma runs straight through the theory chapters. Imagine the
    well-attended consultation where homeowners oppose a modest apartment building —
    authentic voices, authentically heard — while the people who would live in it, and who
    are excluded by the very scarcity the objectors defend, are absent by definition. Pure
    communicative ethics sides with the meeting; pure just-city ethics sides with the
    absent; the rational model points at housing-supply data. Real planners resolve this
    daily with documented judgement — weighing whom the decision actually binds, and owning
    the values doing the weighing — which is why the codes demand honesty about values
    rather than pretending the values are not there.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A city publishes a "vision 2050" built from citizen workshops, then adopts it unchanged after a two-week comment period. Where does this sit on Arnstein's ladder, and what rung would it claim to occupy?</li>
    <li>Why does the rational model work well for a water treatment plant but badly for a neighbourhood rezoning?</li>
    <li>Give one example each of a City Beautiful idea, a garden-city idea and a modernist idea still visible in cities today.</li>
    <li>Fainstein's three criteria can conflict. Describe a concrete case where democracy and equity pull in opposite directions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It sits near the bottom — informing and consultation:
    citizens heard, nothing negotiable. It <em>claims</em> the top rungs (partnership,
    citizen control) because the workshops borrowed the vocabulary of co-creation. The
    tell is that the plan arrived unchanged. (2) The plant has agreed goals, measurable
    alternatives and a technical public interest; the rezoning has conflicting values
    (density versus character, affordability versus asset value) that no dataset resolves
    — exactly Lindblom's and Jacobs's point. (3) Any answers from: monumental civic
    centres and axial boulevards (City Beautiful); ringed greenbelt and self-contained
    planned towns (garden city); separated single-use districts and towers in open space
    (modernism). If you named a current suburb's single-use zoning for modernism —
    correct, and slightly alarming. (4) The classic: the consulted homeowners' meeting
    (democracy, on its face) opposing the housing that non-present future residents need
    (equity). The resolution is not picking a winner but documenting whom the decision
    binds and owning the trade-off — which is the codes' actual demand.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Planning was born sanitary: its charter claim is that urban form determines urban health.</li>
    <li>The profession's movements — City Beautiful, garden cities, zoning, the Athens Charter — each left instruments still in daily use.</li>
    <li>The postwar tests (Brasília, the New Towns, urban renewal) taught the field that clearance destroys social networks that plans never priced.</li>
    <li>The rational model is one tool, not the job: valid when goals are agreed and effects measurable, dangerous when they are not.</li>
    <li>Participation is measured by who decides (Arnstein), not by who attends.</li>
    <li>The just city adds distribution to every appraisal: equity, diversity, democracy — and they trade off.</li>
    <li>Planning ethics does not remove values from decisions; it makes them explicit, documented and defensible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Each tradition here becomes its own later study. Land-use law and zoning practice take
    the legal machinery and its exclusionary history in detail. Urban design deepens
    Jacobs's street-level observation into workable design guidance. Transport and
    infrastructure planning exercise the rational model where it is strongest, and
    environmental planning brings the just-city lens to climate adaptation — which coast
    defends, which retreats, and who decides. The hearing scene from this lesson's opening
    will replay in all of them; what changes is which of the three voices the professional
    is hired to amplify.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper layers complete the theorist's toolkit. The <em>implementation gap</em>:
    plans are promises made in one political moment and kept (or not) across many; scholars
    of the gap study why adopted plans so often die in the budget process, and treat
    planners as street-level bureaucrats whose everyday discretion, not the plan document,
    is where policy actually happens. <em>Informality</em>: most of the world's urban
    growth happens outside formal planning — informal settlements house roughly a billion
    people — and planning theory built for surveyed, zoned cities struggles with, and
    increasingly learns from, incremental self-built urbanism.
  </p>
  <p>
    The newest layer is <em>climate and technology</em>. Adaptation planning introduces
    decisions under deep uncertainty — which coasts to defend, which to retreat from —
    with distributional questions the just-city lens was built for, while the "smart city"
    promises of data-driven management replay the rationalist debate with sensors:
    optimise for whom, and who audits the algorithm? The century's arc — vision,
    technique, critique, conversation, justice — looks likely to repeat itself once more,
    at machine speed.
  </p>

